Exact MP3 reader (Linux and portable builds)
--------------------------------------------
Linux and `--features portable` builds decode MP3 input with
src/portable/audio/mp3/, a project-written Rust implementation (no `unsafe`
code, standard library only, integer arithmetic only) that needs no setting. It
is a compatibility implementation: its purpose is to produce what Apple's
AudioToolbox produces for an MP3 file, and its behavior was recovered from
Apple's own binaries by static analysis, instruction-level transcription and
recorded runtime states of the AudioCodecs component and AudioToolboxCore on
macOS 27.2 (build 26B123) on one reference machine. It therefore contains
numeric constants and algorithms that reproduce Apple's behavior, among them 33
constant tables that are bit-exact copies of data read from the arm64e slice of
AudioCodecs (SHA-256 70e5d4a56b24d037b940328916df496a20267eb206f8e316216ae7bef69a1626);
the table files (tables.rs, back_tables.rs) and the module header (mod.rs) name
the binary and the addresses each table was read from. No Apple binary and no
Apple source code is included, and the reader calls no Apple API. The scripts
that generated and verified the tables, the research corpora and the recorded
Apple results are not part of the public source; the checked-in test fixtures
under tests/fixtures/mp3-exact/ are generated audio with recorded results. This
is a statement of provenance, not a legal opinion, and no legal clearance of
the reader, its constants or its algorithms is claimed. The macOS default build
uses AudioToolbox itself and does not use this reader. An earlier open-source
MP3 decoder written from ISO/IEC 11172-3 and 13818-3 (shipped opt-in in v0.2.0,
with Huffman and window numbers transcribed from the LAME 3.100 archive) was
replaced by this reader and is no longer part of the source.

